# Optional HayChat server overrides for the dev pod (`./dev.sh up` / `./dev.sh restart`).
#
# Usage:
#   cp .env.example .env     # then uncomment what you need and run ./dev.sh restart
#
# Format: KEY=VALUE lines; a leading `export ` is accepted. Only HAYCHAT_* keys are read.
# Point ./dev.sh at another file with HAYCHAT_DEV_ENV_FILE=path.
#
# dev.sh owns the settings that wire the pod together and ignores them here:
#   HAYCHAT_DATABASE_URL, HAYCHAT_DATABASE_PORT, HAYCHAT_DATABASE_USER,
#   HAYCHAT_DATABASE_PASS, HAYCHAT_DATABASE_NAME, HAYCHAT_LISTEN_ADDR, HAYCHAT_LISTEN_PORT,
#   HAYCHAT_LOG_DIR, HAYCHAT_FILE_STORAGE_PATH, HAYCHAT_SERVER_IDENTITY_FILE
# The host port clients connect to is HAYCHAT_DEV_PORT (default 12345), set in your shell.
#
# Running the server outside the pod (`odin run server`) reads the same variables from the
# process environment; see docs/server-configuration.md for every key and its default.

# HAYCHAT_LOG_LEVEL=info
# HAYCHAT_MAX_RESTARTS=5
# HAYCHAT_RESTART_BACKOFF_MIN=0.1
# HAYCHAT_RESTART_BACKOFF_MAX=10.0
# ENet peer capacity: 16 connections per unit (the server no longer starts worker threads).
# HAYCHAT_MAX_NETWORK_THREADS=4

# Voice. It uses the same UDP port as chat; no extra port. Out-of-range values are clamped.
# HAYCHAT_VOICE_ENABLED=true
# Voice sessions per room (1-200). A user has one voice session: a second device takes over.
# HAYCHAT_VOICE_MAX_PARTICIPANTS=25
# Per-connection voice datagram rate (1-1000); clients send 50/s while talking. Burst = rate/3.
# HAYCHAT_VOICE_MAX_PACKETS_PER_SECOND=60
# Largest Opus payload the server relays, in bytes (64-512).
# HAYCHAT_VOICE_MAX_FRAME_BYTES=512

# Attachments (protocols 26-28). Out-of-range values are clamped; see
# docs/server-configuration.md. Back the blob directory up with the database (deploy/README.md).
# false refuses new uploads; stored files stay downloadable.
# HAYCHAT_ATTACHMENTS_ENABLED=true
# Blob directory, relative to the server's working directory (the dev pod's state volume). It
# must not be HAYCHAT_FILE_STORAGE_PATH or hold the server identity file.
# HAYCHAT_ATTACHMENT_DIR=attachments
# Largest stored file (25 MiB; 1 KiB to 4 GiB), stored per account (1 GiB, 0 = none, at most
# 1 TiB), uploaded per account in any hour (512 MiB, 0 = none, at most 1 TiB), and stored on the
# server (0 = none, at most 1 PiB), in bytes.
# HAYCHAT_ATTACHMENT_MAX_BYTES=26214400
# HAYCHAT_ATTACHMENT_ACCOUNT_QUOTA_BYTES=1073741824
# HAYCHAT_ATTACHMENT_UPLOAD_BYTES_PER_HOUR=536870912
# HAYCHAT_ATTACHMENT_CAPACITY_BYTES=0
# Free space the blob directory's volume must keep after an upload (1 GiB; 0 to 1 TiB).
# HAYCHAT_ATTACHMENT_MIN_FREE_BYTES=1073741824
# Unbound uploads are swept after this many hours (1-720).
# HAYCHAT_ATTACHMENT_PENDING_HOURS=24
# Per-connection transfer pace, uploads and downloads together (4 MiB/s; 64 KiB/s to 1 GiB/s).
# HAYCHAT_ATTACHMENT_SESSION_BYTES_PER_SECOND=4194304
# One start only: let the startup scan delete files without a row although the attachments
# table is empty or more than 10% of the files (and more than 10) have none. Remove it after
# that start. See deploy/README.md (Restoring).
# HAYCHAT_ATTACHMENT_ALLOW_ORPHAN_PURGE=false

# Hex Ed25519 public key allowed to claim keyless accounts that hold server roles (for
# example the seeded `admin`). Without it, privileged seeded accounts cannot be claimed over
# the network. For a dev client profile, read it with:
#   ./dev.sh key [PROFILE]
# HAYCHAT_ADMIN_PUBLIC_KEY=

# Fixed server identity. Leave unset: the server generates a key in its state volume and
# keeps it until ./dev.sh reset.
# HAYCHAT_SERVER_PRIVATE_KEY=
# HAYCHAT_SERVER_PUBLIC_KEY=
